= The Consumer Protection Act, 2019: Legal Architecture

The Consumer Protection Act, 2019 (CPA 2019) was enacted to modernize consumer law, repealing the earlier Consumer Protection Act, 1986. It broadens the scope of consumer rights to encompass digital e-commerce, direct selling, and telemarketing.

== Legal Definition of a 'Consumer' (Section 2(7) of CPA 2019)
Under the Act, a consumer is defined as:
1. Any person who buys any goods for a consideration which has been paid or promised, or partly paid and partly promised, or under any system of deferred payment. Includes any user of such goods when such use is made with the approval of the buyer.
2. Any person who hires or avails of any service for a consideration paid, promised, or deferred. Includes any beneficiary of such service when availed with the approval of the hirer.

=== Critical Exclusion (Non-Consumer)
The legal definition strictly excludes any person who obtains goods for *resale* or for any *commercial purpose*.
- *The Self-Employment Livelihood Exception:* If an individual purchases commercial machinery (e.g., a commercial photocopier or a transport taxi) exclusively for the purpose of earning their personal livelihood through *self-employment*, they remain legally classified as a consumer.

== Legal Standing: Who Can File a Formal Complaint?
Under Section 35 of CPA 2019, a complaint can be filed by:
- Any consumer individually.
- Any voluntary consumer association registered under the Societies Registration Act or the Companies Act.
- The Central Government or any State Government.
- The Central Consumer Protection Authority (CCPA).
- One or more consumers, where there are numerous consumers having the same common interest (class action).
- In case of death of a consumer, their legal heir or recognized legal representative.
- In case of a minor consumer, their parent or legal guardian.

= The Six Statutory Consumer Rights

Section 2(9) of the Consumer Protection Act, 2019 guarantees six foundational consumer rights:

== 1. Right to Safety
The right to be protected against the marketing of goods and services that are hazardous to life, health, or property.
- *Practical Implication:* Requires consumers to purchase standardized goods carrying certified safety marks (e.g., ISI mark on pressure cookers and electrical appliances).

== 2. Right to be Informed
The right to be informed about the quality, quantity, potency, purity, standard, and price of goods or services.
- *Practical Implication:* Protects consumers against unfair trade practices and false claims. Mandates comprehensive disclosure on product packages: ingredients, manufacturing date, expiry date, batch number, direction for use, and Maximum Retail Price (MRP).

== 3. Right to Choose (Right to Assured Access)
The right to be assured of access to a variety of goods and services at competitive prices wherever possible.
- *Practical Implication:* Precludes monopolistic coercion, tied selling (forcing a buyer to purchase insurance when buying a car), or aggressive vendor manipulation.

== 4. Right to be Heard
The right to receive due consideration and fair hearing in appropriate consumer dispute redressal forums.
- *Practical Implication:* Mandates that corporate enterprises establish internal customer care desks and consumer grievance handling departments.

== 5. Right to Seek Redressal
The right to seek fair legal remedy against unfair trade practices, restrictive trade practices, or exploitation.
- *Practical Implication:* Confers rights to secure product repair, replacement, price refund, or monetary compensation for damages.

== 6. Right to Consumer Education
The right to acquire the knowledge and skills necessary to remain an informed, vigilant consumer throughout life.
- *Practical Implication:* Government consumer welfare initiatives like the *Jago Grahak Jago* campaign and consumer literacy curricula in schools.

