== Part 3: Advanced Evaluative & Real-World Vignettes (Q11 to Q15)

#mcq(11, [Under the 'Product Liability' provisions of the Consumer Protection Act, 2019, who among the following can be held legally liable to compensate an aggrieved consumer for harm, personal injury, or property damage caused by a defective product?], (
  [Only the retail shopkeeper who sold the product],
  [Only the advertising agency that designed the promotion],
  [Only the local municipal trade licensing authority],
  [The product manufacturer, product service provider, or product seller],
))
#solution(11, [Product Liability under CPA 2019:
- Under Chapter VI of CPA 2019, *Product Liability* holds the *product manufacturer, product service provider, or product seller* strictly liable to compensate consumers for physical injury, death, or property damage caused by defective products or deficient services.
Therefore, Option D accurately identifies the liable parties.], answer: [D])

#mcq(12, [Consider the following two statements regarding Consumer Redressal under CPA 2019:
Statement I: Any consumer aggrieved by an order of the District Commission can appeal to the State Commission within 45 days from the date of the order.
Statement II: An order passed by a State Commission in an appeal filed against a District Commission order can be appealed directly to the Supreme Court of India.
Choose the correct option:], (
  [Statement I is false, Statement II is true],
  [Statement I is true, Statement II is false],
  [Both Statement I and Statement II are true],
  [Both Statement I and Statement II are false],
))
#solution(12, [Evaluating Redressal Appeal Procedures:
- Statement I is true: Appeals from the District Commission to the State Commission must be filed within *45 days* under CPA 2019.
- Statement II is false: An appeal against an order of the State Commission lies to the *National Commission* (within 30 days), NOT directly to the Supreme Court. Appeal to the Supreme Court lies only against *original orders* of the National Commission.
Therefore, *Statement I is true, Statement II is false* (Option B).], answer: [B])

#mcq(13, [A voluntary consumer organization (NGO) named 'Consumer Voice' conducts independent laboratory testing on several brands of packaged drinking water, discovers dangerous bacterial contamination in three brands, and publishes the findings in its monthly consumer magazine. Which core role of consumer NGOs is exemplified here?], (
  [Accelerating consumer awareness and testing product quality in accredited laboratories],
  [Exercising judicial powers to arrest company executives],
  [Issuing mandatory ISI certification licenses to manufacturers],
  [Fixing the retail prices of packaged drinking water across India],
))
#solution(13, [Role of Consumer NGOs:
- Consumer organizations perform critical public welfare functions, including *testing consumer products in independent accredited laboratories, publishing comparative test results*, and educating the public.
- They do not possess judicial powers or statutory licensing authority.
Therefore, this exemplifies *testing product quality and accelerating consumer awareness* (Option A).], answer: [A])

#mcq(14, [A consumer buys an expensive smartphone. Due to a manufacturing motherboard defect, the phone overheats and stops functioning. The consumer approaches the District Commission. Which of the following is *NOT* an admissible relief that the Commission can grant under CPA 2019?], (
  [Direct the company to repair and remove the defect from the smartphone],
  [Direct the company to replace the smartphone with a new, defect-free unit],
  [Order the immediate permanent dissolution and nationalization of the smartphone manufacturing corporation],
  [Direct the company to refund the full purchase price along with interest],
))
#solution(14, [Reliefs Available under CPA 2019:
- Admissible remedies include: removal of defect, replacement of product, refund of price, payment of compensation, and punitive damages.
- Consumer commissions do NOT possess the statutory authority to order the *dissolution or nationalization of a corporate enterprise*.
Therefore, Option C is NOT an admissible relief.], answer: [C])

#mcq(15, [Under the Consumer Protection Act, 2019, if a celebrity knowingly endorses a product in a misleading advertisement that falsely claims to cure chronic illnesses, what statutory penalty can the Central Consumer Protection Authority (CCPA) impose on the endorser?], (
  [The CCPA can only issue an informal verbal warning with no monetary penalty],
  [The CCPA can permanently confiscate all personal assets of the celebrity],
  [The CCPA has no jurisdiction over celebrity endorsers],
  [The CCPA can impose a fine up to ₹10 Lakhs (and up to ₹50 Lakhs for repeat violations) and prohibit the celebrity from endorsing any product for up to one year],
))
#solution(15, [CCPA Penalties on Misleading Advertisements and Endorsers:
- Under Section 21 of CPA 2019, the CCPA can impose a penalty of *up to ₹10 Lakhs* on a manufacturer or endorser for misleading advertisements.
- For repeat violations, the penalty can extend *up to ₹50 Lakhs*, and the celebrity endorser can be prohibited from making any product endorsement for up to one year (and up to three years for subsequent offenses).
Therefore, Option D accurately describes the statutory penalty.], answer: [D])
